<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-Soviet split</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-Soviet split was the breakdown of relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Beginning with ideological disputes after Khrushchev's denunciation of Stalin in 1956, it led to the withdrawal of Soviet advisers in 1960 and an open break by the early 1960s, dividing the world communist movement and culminating in border clashes in 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split.jpg" alt="Sino-Soviet split" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographerUnknown photographer at the source. Photo distributed by United Press International from files. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Ts%C3%A9-toung,_portrait_en_buste,_assis,_faisant_face_%C3%A0_Nikita_Khrouchtchev,_pendant_la_visite_du_chef_russe_1958_%C3%A0_P%C3%A9kin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> to cooperate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and after Japan's surrender the Chinese civil war resumed and the communists won by 1949. In 1950 Mao and Stalin signed the Treaty of Friendship, Alliance and Mutual Assistance. Stalin gave a loan of 300 million dollars, promised military aid should Japan attack, and handed over the Chinese Eastern Railway, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalian">Dalian</a>; in return the People's Republic recognised the independence of the Mongolian People's Republic. Unlike the satellite states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was not controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>In early 1956 relations began to deteriorate after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> denounced Stalin in his speech On the Cult of Personality and Its Consequences. His turn from confrontation with the West to peaceful coexistence posed problems of credibility and authority for Mao, who had modelled his leadership on Stalin's. Mao called the Soviet policy revisionism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1958 Chinese domestic politics took on an anti-Soviet tone, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> became more sensitive about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and foreign policy, especially over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. In 1959 Khrushchev met US President Dwight Eisenhower, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> reneged on an agreement for technical aid for China's atomic bomb project, and it sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the border dispute. In 1960 Mao expected Khrushchev to hold Eisenhower to account over a shot-down U-2 spy plane, and when Khrushchev did not win an apology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> summit, the Chinese held rallies demanding military confrontation. By 1961 China formally denounced Soviet communism as the work of revisionist traitors.</p><p>From then communications were written. In June 1963 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> published its proposal on the general line of the international communist movement, attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> by name, and the Soviets replied with an Open Letter. Mao launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> in 1966, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-china">Red Guards</a> harassed the Soviet embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhenbao-island">Zhenbao Island</a> border incident of 1969 the Soviet Union reportedly considered a large-scale nuclear strike against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the Chinese leadership was evacuated from Beijing before both sides returned to negotiations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Talks over border issues began in October 1969 and lasted a decade. Both sides built up forces along the frontier, with an estimated 1 million to 1.2 million Soviet troops and as many as 1.5 million Chinese. The split turned the bipolar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> into a tripolar one and helped make possible President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a>'s visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1972. It also weakened the Western idea of monolithic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. In the 1970s the two states competed for influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, though both supported North Vietnam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sino-Soviet split” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Sino-Soviet split</b>, open by the early 1960s, divided the communist world, and Mao’s doctrine of rural revolution inspired movements from India’s Naxalites to Peru’s Shining Path, while China backed communist forces in Vietnam and the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Sino-Soviet split</b> of the 1960s each party claimed to be Stalin’s sole heir, and from 1962 China’s challenge to Soviet leadership divided communist parties around the world.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
